1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,480
Hi. In this video, we'll show you how you can use Burp to test for SQL injection vulnerabilities.

2
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:17,840
SQL injection vulnerabilities occur when an attacker can interfere with the queries that

3
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:22,880
an application makes to its database. You can use Burp Scanner to automatically flag

4
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:29,600
potential vulnerabilities, or use Burp Intruder to insert a list of SQL fuzz strings into a request.

5
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:36,320
This may enable you to change the way SQL commands are executed. If you'd like to follow along,

6
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:42,720
you can use the lab SQL injection vulnerability in where clause allowing retrieval of hidden data.

7
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:49,760
First, we'll look at using Burp Scanner. Once the lab has launched, identify a request you want to

8
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:57,440
investigate. Here, from the proxy HTTP history tab, we can right click the request and select

9
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:02,800
Do Active Scan. We can then go to the dashboard and review the Issue Activity panel,

10
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:10,320
or click on View Detail for the specific scan task, and identify any SQL injection vulnerabilities

11
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:18,400
Burp Scanner has flagged. Alternatively, you can use Burp Intruder to test for SQL injection vulnerabilities.

12
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:24,160
This process also allows you to closely investigate any issues identified by Burp Scanner.

13
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:35,040
In the request, highlight the parameter you want to test, right click, and choose Send to Intruder.

14
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:42,960
Go to the Intruder Positions tab. The parameter you highlighted will have been automatically marked as a

15
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:51,520
payload position. Go to the Payloads tab. Under Payload Settings, you can add a list of fuzz strings.

16
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:58,480
If you're using Burp Suite Professional, you have the option of using the built-in Fuzzing SQL Word List.

17
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:02,560
If you're using Community, you will need to manually add your own list.

18
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:08,320
Under Payload Processing, click Add to configure appropriate values for the placeholders if you're

19
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:15,920
using the built-in list. To replace the base placeholder, select Replace Placeholder Base Value.

20
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:25,040
To replace other placeholders, select Match Replace and specify the details you want to change.

21
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:29,920
For example, replace Domain with the domain name of the site you're testing.

22
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:37,920
Click Start Attack.

23
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:48,480
Once the attack has finished, study the responses for interesting behaviour, such as responses that

24
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:54,800
include additional data as a result of the query, or responses that include other differences due to

25
00:02:54,800 --> 00:03:00,800
the query, such as a welcome back message or an error message, or responses that had a time delay due to the query.

26
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:07,200
If you're using the lab, look for responses with a longer length.

27
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:09,680
These may include additional products.

